<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      THE PRERELEASE SERVICE ADMINISTRATIVE HELPER.

      SECURITY CLASSIFICATION. What this project builds is UNSIGNED PRERELEASE
      CODE. It is accepted only for internal prerelease development and attended
      validation. It is NOT tamper resistant, NOT integrity trusted, NOT
      production ready and NOT customer safe. An unsigned helper does not resist
      replacement by a local user, UAC may show an unknown publisher, and
      prerelease validation proves functionality only - never production tamper
      resistance. This is unacceptable for GA. GA activation stays BLOCKED until
      this exact helper is Authenticode signed and the expected publisher policy
      is configured and verified (cycle-60 ruling).

      SHAPE. Console Exe, published SELF-CONTAINED and directly launched, so the
      trust boundary can later be the outer Authenticode signature on this PE
      rather than dotnet.exe loading a user-writable DLL. NativeAOT is NOT used.

      net8.0-windows is required because the compile-linked cycle-58/59 sources
      use PipeSecurity, WindowsIdentity and System.Security.AccessControl. This
      project deliberately does NOT set UseWindowsForms/UseWPF, so it resolves
      only the Microsoft.NETCore.App framework - no WinForms, no desktop runtime
      pack, and no App/UI/MSAL/WebView2/PAX/Cook dependency of any kind.
    -->
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>PAXCookbook.ServiceAdminHelper</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>PAXCookbookServiceAdminHelper</AssemblyName>
    <UseWindowsForms>false</UseWindowsForms>
    <UseWPF>false</UseWPF>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <EnableDefaultNoneItems>false</EnableDefaultNoneItems>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    PRERELEASE UNSIGNED ALLOWANCE - EXPLICIT BUILD-TIME OPT-IN ONLY.

    Follows the existing TestIsolation / ManagedInventoryProvisioning
    conditional-DefineConstants precedent in PAXCookbookSetup.csproj. A normal
    or default build defines NOTHING here, so the signing policy never returns
    an unsigned allowance and elevation can never be silently permitted. There
    is deliberately NO environment variable, NO command-line argument, NO
    registry value and NO configuration file that can turn this on at runtime:
    the ONLY switch is /p:PrereleaseUnsignedHelper=true at compile time.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PrereleaseUnsignedHelper)' == 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);PAXCOOKBOOK_PRERELEASE_UNSIGNED_HELPER</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="PAXCookbook.ServiceAdminHelper.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE EMBEDDED DETERMINISTIC SERVICE PAYLOAD.

    Supplied by tools\release\Build-Setup.ps1 as /p:ServicePayloadArchive=<path>.
    The logical name is FIXED and is the single name the verifier will accept;
    it is declared once in ServicePayloadArchiveFormat.ResourceName and must be
    kept byte-identical with the LogicalName below. A build without the property
    produces a helper with NO payload, which the verifier reports as a bounded
    ResourceMissing refusal rather than any kind of success.

    This inner archive + manifest is ONLY a corruption/consistency check. It is
    NOT a tamper check and can never be one: the checker travels inside the
    container it validates (cycle-60 finding). The outer Authenticode signature
    is the trust anchor, and it does not exist yet.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ServicePayloadArchive)' != ''">
    <EmbeddedResource Include="$(ServicePayloadArchive)" LogicalName="PAXCookbook.ServicePayload.zip" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    COMPILE-LINKED CYCLE-58 / CYCLE-59 SOURCES.

    ONE source implementation, not copied code, exactly as PAXCookbook.Service
    already links its Shared contracts. A ProjectReference to PAXCookbookSetup
    is PROHIBITED here: that project is WinExe + UseWindowsForms and would drag
    WinForms and the whole installer surface into this helper.

    The set below is the audited TRANSITIVE closure required by
    ServiceInstallationAnchorStore, ServiceInitiatingUserIdentityChannel and
    ServiceInitiatorProcessBinding, plus the cycle-61 sibling launch resolver:
      * those three Setup service sources themselves;
      * ServiceAdminHelperLocationResolver, whose natural home is Setup (Setup
        is what launches the helper) and which is linked here ONLY so the
        focused tests can exercise it - one source, two assemblies;
      * ServiceInstallationAnchorContract (the anchor schema they validate against);
      * ServiceMachineStorageContract (the fixed machine path/leaf names);
      * ServiceOwnershipLedgerContract, which is required ONLY because
        ServiceMachineStorageContract.OwnershipLedgerFileName is a const derived
        from ServiceOwnershipLedgerContract.LedgerFileName. It is a pure string
        validator: no I/O, no certificate store, no registry, no key access.
    Nothing here reads or writes the ownership ledger, and no ledger reader,
    lifecycle planner, credential observer, elevation coordinator or
    elevated-helper dispatch is linked.

    CYCLE 62 ADDITIONS, each with a named reason:
      * ServiceEnableProtocol - the ONE source authority for the service-enable
        grammar. Linking it is what stops Setup and this helper drifting into
        two spellings of the same verb, option and token count.
      * ServiceSidResolver - the EXISTING fixed name-to-SID lookup for
        NT SERVICE\PAXCookbookService. It is linked so the transaction can
        resolve the service SID after creation WITHOUT shelling out to
        `sc showsid` and WITHOUT reimplementing the derivation algorithm. It is
        a read-only two-call lookup: no certificate store, no key, no registry,
        no ACL, no credential vault and no service control.
      * ServiceIdentityContract - the fixed service name, display name and
        account name that ServiceSidResolver and the SCM adapter both require.

    CYCLE 63R ADDITION:
      * ServiceDisableProtocol - the ONE source authority for the service-disable
        grammar, linked for exactly the same reason as ServiceEnableProtocol so
        the initiator and the elevated helper can never drift into two spellings
        of the same verb, option or token count. It is pure: no I/O, no process
        start, no elevation, no service control and no override option.

    CYCLE 67 ADDITION:
      * ServiceEnableFailureContract - the ONE source authority for the bounded
        failure CAUSE vocabulary, the separate DISPOSITION vocabulary, the
        helper exit-code band and the fixed lowercase diagnostic tokens. It is
        linked for exactly the same reason as ServiceEnableProtocol: the
        elevated helper computes the exit code and the non-elevated coordinator
        interprets it, so a second, drifting copy of that arithmetic would be a
        silent cross-process contract break. It is pure: no I/O, no process
        start, no elevation, no service control and no identity.

    CYCLE 94 (PASS C) ADDITIONS - THE AUDITED OWNERSHIP CLOSURE.

    THIS IS THE EXPANSION CYCLE 89 SAID WOULD HAVE TO BE AUDITED, NOT A SIDE
    EFFECT. Cycle 89 declined to link the promotion request parser into this
    process and pinned a transport literal instead, stating plainly that linking
    it "is a real expansion of that process's attack surface and is an audited
    decision". Pass C makes that decision deliberately: the elevated helper is
    the ONLY process that can run an ownership transaction, so the ONE
    composition root and everything it directly names must compile here. Each
    source below is linked EXACTLY ONCE, and each has a named reason.

      * ServiceOwnershipElevatedProtocol - the ONE source authority for the two
        ownership verbs and their closed grammar, linked for exactly the same
        reason as ServiceEnableProtocol and ServiceDisableProtocol.
      * ServiceOwnershipElevatedTransaction - THE ONE AUTHORIZED COMPOSITION
        ROOT. It is the only production implementation of the payload-bearing
        transaction seam, so the helper must compile the one implementation
        rather than a copy.
      * ServicePromotionRequestContract - the closed two-verb request parser the
        composition root calls. Linking it is what lets the transport ceiling in
        ServiceInitiatingUserIdentityChannel stop being a retyped literal and
        become a direct derivation from MaxRequestChars.
      * ServiceOwnershipLedgerReader / ServiceOwnershipLedgerWriter - the fixed
        read and durable-write surfaces, plus the fixed persistence adapter that
        a certified containment guard requires to live inside the writer file.
      * ServiceOwnershipPromotionExecutor - the ordered transaction itself.
      * ServiceOwnershipPromotedRecipeStore - the fixed promoted-Recipe store and
        the fixed port that a containment guard requires to live beside it.
      * ServiceOwnershipCredentialObserver - the fixed credential observation
        shim and the certified entry observation adapter, which the same kind of
        containment guard requires to live inside that file.
      * ServiceOwnershipFixedOwnerIdentityAdapter,
        ServiceOwnershipFixedCertificateFactsAdapter and
        ServiceOwnershipFixedKeyDescriptorAdapters - the fixed native adapters
        the composition root constructs.
      * ServiceOwnershipLedgerSerializer, ServiceOwnershipTransitionAuthority and
        ServiceOwnershipLifecyclePlanner - the certified serialization,
        transition and planning authorities. All three are PURE: no I/O, no
        certificate store, no registry, no key access and no service control.
      * RecipeValidationModel and JsonModel - the SHARED SOURCE the request
        parser's Recipe gate already uses, compiled in place exactly as
        PAXCookbookSetup already compiles them, so a Recipe accepted here is
        accepted identically on the desktop side with no second implementation.
      * OrganizationKeyIdentifierPattern and PaxAdapter.Validation - the two
        remaining pieces of that same shared source the Recipe gate binds. The
        first is ONE regular-expression constant. The second is a PARTIAL that
        deliberately carries ONLY the validator's removed-switch refusal: it has
        no argv projection, no secret-shape scan and no engine acquisition, which
        is precisely why PAXCookbookSetup already compiles it in isolation. NO
        PAX ENGINE, PAX INVOCATION OR COOK PATH ENTERS THIS PROCESS THROUGH IT.

    WHAT IS STILL NOT LINKED, AND STAYS THAT WAY. No WinForms, WPF, MSAL,
    WebView2, PAX, Cook, App, UI, installer, coordinator or elevation-launch
    source is here, and there is still NO ProjectReference to PAXCookbookSetup,
    PAXCookbook.App or PAXCookbook.Shared.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\PAXCookbookSetup\Service\ServiceInstallationAnchorStore.cs"
             Link="Linked\Service\ServiceInstallationAnchorStore.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\PAXCookbookSetup\Service\ServiceEnableFailureContract.cs"
             Link="Linked\Service\ServiceEnableFailureContract.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\PAXCookbookSetup\Service\ServiceInitiatingUserIdentityChannel.cs"
